Z||000003||Ronnie||06-22-2001||08:40 AM||||If you ever see a DOHC 3.4 , you will know the diff immediately. The plug wires go directly into the valve covers like a hemi engine. Plus , the valve covers are about twice as wide as the OHV engine covers. I had one in a 93 Olds Cutlass International convertible.||||reg||6
Z||000004||Alex4mula||06-22-2001||11:29 AM||||I have rev mine to 6300 RPM. Stock cam/valvetrain, Accel coil & Jacobs wires. I personaly wouldn't do over that.<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||06-24-2001||03:53 PM||||I believe they list the redline at 5500.  I never take it past that, 60 degree V6s are notorious for broken rods and spun bearings.<P>About DOHC, your thinking of the DOHC put in Monte Carlo Z34's from 91-96 I believe.  It was a nice motor.  People have put them in Fiero's also.  GM is putting an inline DOHC 6 cyl. in their new TrailBlazer.  They also have the Northstar and shortstar.  The SBC is probably next to go.||||reg||1
